1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,149
Lesson 5.7.

2
00:00:02,529 --> 00:00:04,030
Hardware abstractions.

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:07,362
An Ising model with every possible

4
00:00:07,362 --> 00:00:10,354
connection has one
coupling per pair of spins.

5
00:00:10,734 --> 00:00:14,163
With ten thousand spins
that is almost fifty million

6
00:00:14,163 --> 00:00:17,591
couplings, and each one
needs a wire and a register.

7
00:00:17,971 --> 00:00:19,816
No chip can wire that.

8
00:00:20,196 --> 00:00:22,887
That is why hardware
offers a fixed, sparse

9
00:00:22,887 --> 00:00:25,391
graph, and programs have to adapt to it.

10
00:00:26,517 --> 00:00:32,956
A complete graph on N nodes has N(N
− 1)/2 edges: it grows as the square.

11
00:00:33,336 --> 00:00:37,737
A graph of fixed degree d
has N d/2: it grows linearly.

12
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:41,168
For ten thousand nodes and
degree sixteen, that is

13
00:00:41,168 --> 00:00:44,037
eighty thousand edges
instead of fifty million.

14
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:47,934
Moreover, on a chip the
connections must be short, so

15
00:00:47,934 --> 00:00:50,987
each pbit's neighbors
are nearby in the plane.

16
00:00:51,367 --> 00:00:53,687
That fixed graph is
called the parent graph.

17
00:00:54,817 --> 00:00:59,051
What if a model variable needs more
neighbors than the hardware offers?

18
00:00:59,431 --> 00:01:03,811
We represent it with a chain of
physical pbits joined by strong

19
00:01:03,811 --> 00:01:08,123
ferromagnetic couplings, so that
they behave as a single spin.

20
00:01:08,503 --> 00:01:11,108
Each copy contributes
its own free neighbors.

21
00:01:11,488 --> 00:01:13,801
With degree sixteen, a variable with

22
00:01:13,801 --> 00:01:17,015
ninety-nine neighbors needs
at least seven copies.

23
00:01:17,395 --> 00:01:19,350
This is called minor embedding.

24
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:24,413
The chain coupling must be
strong, because if the copies

25
00:01:24,413 --> 00:01:28,801
disagree, the logical variable
is undefined: the chain breaks.

26
00:01:29,181 --> 00:01:35,464
For two isolated copies, the breaking
probability is 1/(1 + e^(2βJ_c)).

27
00:01:35,844 --> 00:01:40,067
With βJ_c = 1 it is twelve
percent; with two, under

28
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:44,042
two percent; with three,
a quarter of a percent.

29
00:01:44,422 --> 00:01:49,070
But a coupling that is too strong
freezes the chain and slows mixing.

30
00:01:49,450 --> 00:01:50,334
It has to be balanced.

31
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:55,624
The cost of embedding is the number of
physical pbits per logical variable.

32
00:01:56,004 --> 00:02:01,242
For sparse models of degree below sixteen,
it is one: the model fits directly.

33
00:02:01,622 --> 00:02:04,190
For degree thirty-one, three copies.

34
00:02:04,570 --> 00:02:09,212
For a dense model of a thousand variables,
each with nine hundred ninety-nine

35
00:02:09,212 --> 00:02:13,853
neighbors, at least seventy-two copies
per variable are needed, plus routing.

36
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:18,787
That is why algorithms for thermodynamic
hardware are designed sparse from

37
00:02:18,787 --> 00:02:22,910
the start, as we will see with the
sparse transformers of unit ten.

38
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:25,402
Let's compute with the Z1.

39
00:02:25,782 --> 00:02:28,214
Its two hundred sixty-nine thousand

40
00:02:28,214 --> 00:02:31,966
degree-sixteen pbits have
about two million couplings.

41
00:02:32,346 --> 00:02:35,345
A sparse degree-sixteen
model fills the whole chip

42
00:02:35,345 --> 00:02:38,164
with two hundred sixty-nine
thousand variables.

43
00:02:38,544 --> 00:02:43,020
A dense model, in contrast, with seventy-two
copies per variable, would barely

44
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:47,210
reach about three thousand seven
hundred variables, not counting routing.

45
00:02:48,317 --> 00:02:52,436
The design rule is to choose
sparse, local models from the start,

46
00:02:52,436 --> 00:02:56,238
and to embed with chains only
the few high-degree variables.

47
00:02:56,618 --> 00:03:00,814
The coloring of the parent graph sets
how many clock phases a sweep takes.

48
00:03:01,194 --> 00:03:06,610
In lab eight you will embed a small
dense model into a degree-sixteen graph.

49
00:03:07,717 --> 00:03:11,690
Common mistakes: assuming
any model runs directly.

50
00:03:12,070 --> 00:03:15,254
Ignoring broken chains
when decoding samples.

51
00:03:15,634 --> 00:03:19,336
Using chain couplings so
strong that mixing freezes.

52
00:03:19,716 --> 00:03:22,091
And counting the copies
without counting the routing.

53
00:03:23,217 --> 00:03:27,735
In summary: full connectivity
grows as N² and cannot be

54
00:03:27,735 --> 00:03:32,253
wired, so hardware offers a
fixed, sparse parent graph.

55
00:03:32,633 --> 00:03:36,122
High-degree variables are
embedded as chains of coupled

56
00:03:36,122 --> 00:03:39,676
copies, with a strength that
balances breaks and mixing.

57
00:03:40,056 --> 00:03:44,239
The cost of embedding pushes toward
designing sparse models from the start.

58
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:48,505
To practice: count the
edges of both graphs.

59
00:03:48,885 --> 00:03:51,441
Compute the minimum
copies for a given degree.

60
00:03:51,821 --> 00:03:54,560
Compute the breaking
probability of a chain.

61
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:59,028
And explain why a chain that is
too strong makes sampling slow.
